Wrenbury Market
[vignette dungeons/wrenbury]

FIXME(mundane): PC arrives at a battlefield relic market in Wrenbury village. Food stalls at the entrance: a vendor with a portable clay oven and flat paddle selling meat pies wrapped in waxed cloth; next to him, a woman selling small beer from a worn cask.
FIXME(mundane): Past the food, blanket stalls selling battle relics. One blanket: a grid of rust-pitted spearheads and arrowpoints on dark cloth, each piece labeled with a handwritten card noting origin and unit. Next blanket: buttons, buckles, a dented canteen with something scratched into the side — possibly a name. No cards for these.
FIXME(mundane): One stall breaks the pattern: Revathi tile fragments, sorted by color — blues, ochres, an unusual green. Clearly genuine. Wrong battlefield, wrong century, no obvious explanation for their presence.
FIXME(mundane): End of the row: a stall of military histories and map prints. The bookseller has the face of a man who has spent years telling people not to touch things. He watches every hand near his stock.
FIXME(dread): Raised voices past him. A young woman leans over a blanket at a seated vendor — rigid composure that follows a first shout. He's leaning back, palms up. Bystanders have slowed to watch.
FIXME(dread): The woman clocks the PC's ring. PC is a Guild arbiter; the ring marks them as one and locals recognize it. She holds the PC's gaze — doesn't call out yet, but the implicit offer is there: walk over and you're no longer a tourist.
